\startchapter[reference=intro, title=Introduction]
% Basic structure: Zoom in; What’s the big field, how to get from there to scRNA-Seq?

% big topic: cell biology and medicine
Cell biology is the science touching the most existential part of us.
Every aspect of our health and therefore continued existence as thinking beings touches cell function.
Our body consists of cells performing the most varied tasks in order to maintain it:
even our thinking depends on neurons, our psyche on cellular regulation of our brain chemistry.
Formed by evolution, this machinery is not built to last.
Health, life and death are therefore topics every human has to confront at some point in their life.

% complex diseases need better tools
Modern medicine has performed incredible feats of extending our expected lifespan and living quality.
In our consequently aging society, it becomes increasingly important to understand the long term effects of living.
It is also possible to shift our focus to rarer or more complex diseases as the low hanging fruit is being reaped.
This includes diseases like cancer, caused by mutated cells, or diseases caused by malfunctioning regulations.
All those diseases have in common that they cannot be understood without knowing the cellular mechanisms involved.

% motivate RNA-Seq
All inner workings of a cell are performed directly or indirectly through proteins.
Knowing what proteins are being produced in a cell at a given moment allows to draw conclusions about its purpose and health.
According to the central dogma of molecular biology, the current production of a given protein is indicated by the presence of the short lived messenger RNA (mRNA), transcribed from the DNA and containing a blueprint ready to be translated into the protein.%
\footnote{Or rather a class of proteins: Similar proteins come from the same gene which is transcribed into a pre-mRNA and then spliced into one of several possible final mRNAs encoding a different protein each.}
It is possible to quantify and subsequently analyze snapshots of all mRNA molecules in flight using RNA sequencing (RNA-Seq).
This is called gene expression analysis.
Decades of biological research made due with averaged RNA profiles of cell samples (bulk RNA-Seq).
Those allow to capture the gene expression of a tissue or fluid sample without accounting for cell-to-cell variability.

\startplacefigure[reference=fig:dogma, location=left, title={The central dogma}]
\externalfigure[https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Centraldogma_nodetails.png][width=3cm]
\stopplacefigure

% SC revolution (cite nat meth of the year 20xx) this one? https://www.nature.com/articles/nmeth.1557
But Biology is immensely complex, so every answer seems to only bring forth new questions:
e.\,g. the confidently named central dogma of molecular biology continues to accumulate exceptions and neither genes nor cell types are considered discrete, enumerable entities anymore (see \infig[fig:dogma]).
More complex diseases require a more intricate understanding of cellular processes and inter-cellular variation.
Advances in experimental technology of the last decade allow to tackle these challenges.
Single-cell RNA-Seq (scRNA-Seq) can measure extremely small expression values down to a single mRNA molecule.
\TODO[the following two are a bit redundant, make clear what you mean]
This data comes not without its own challenges, but enables the study of heterogeneous tissues like cancer, where expression averages from bulk RNA-Seq are not meaningful.
The second great advantage is that the amounts of data present in scRNA-Seq lend themselves to machine learning approaches:
patterns that no human can detect by hand and careful choice of samples can be derived from the ample variation between hundreds to millions of individual cells.
\TODO[highlight a few: m. rajewsky work, teichman & regev papers]

%challenges
All these opportunities come at the cost of additional complexity and more confounding variables.
The small amount of mRNA molecules in single cells results in noisier measurements and the so-called drop-out or zero inflation effect:
genes with the smallest counts tend to appear as zeros the data.
The increased complexity can be conquered with improved modelling or more sophisticated preprocessing.
The modelling approach is more correct, but less flexible, as it requires careful informed design across the whole analysis process.
Each new analysis that significantly deviates from previous analyses requires a re-thinking and re-building of the model almost from the ground up.

% my work
The more practical approach is to start with careful improvements in preprocessing that allow to shape the data in a way that fullfills the assumptions of subsequent analysis.
The second step is to create and use streamlined building blocks for pipelines to preprocess and analyze the data, and to then then visualize the insights gained.
Until few years ago, there were no freely available programming and environments that allowed this approach for single cell data analysis.
Analysis pipelines came custom-built for a certain publication, just as hard to take apart and reuse as a holistic model would have been.
Every graduate student and postdoctoral researcher had to start from scratch.
The thesis at hand is a slice of the author’s continued efforts to change this.
I improved interoperability and modularity of existing tools, pioneered technologies and software development processes in a research environment, and developed some of the missing building blocks for the resulting toolkit.

\startsection[title=Workflow]
% story: new challenges by sc data. old way to deal with data (workflows) are not efficient. new approaches needed → me

\startsubsection[title=Overview]
% Describe how the workflows work and why the aforementioned is important.
The general workflow of scRNA-Seq data processing consists of five steps.

% 1. library preparation (cells → transcript library) and sequencing (transcript library → reads)
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:rna-seq,title={Library preparation, sequencing, and mapping}]
% https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Summary_of_RNA-Seq.svg
\externalfigure[Summary_of_RNA-Seq.svg][width=.7\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

It begins with an experiment to prepare and sequence a transcript library containing mostly mature mRNA but also unspliced mRNA and other RNA (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]a).
There are different kinds of library preparation protocols, all of which have in common that they isolate and fragment the RNA per single cell, then tag each fragment with a cell specific barcode for later assignment to their origin cell.
The most important differences between protocols manifest in cost, sensitivity and the distinction between those capturing paired fragments starting at both ends of the RNA and those capturing only the RNA end where translation into proteins starts (the 3’ end).
The sequencing machine finally digitizes the fragments and allows all further processing to happen on a computer (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]b).
\TODO[give figure an a, b, c. Mention droplet stuff here?]
\cite[ziegenhain2017,haque2017,huang2018]
% 1½. protocol specific bias correction
Additional to the cell barcode, some protocols allow to enrich the library with information that can be used to reduce sources of noise or bias.
This includes e.\,g. unique molecular identifiers (UMIs), which allows to count each transcript only once despite a common protocol step involving several duplications of all transcripts.
A second example is adding known quantities of RNA from a foreign organism (spike-in RNA) to each cell library, which later allows to correct for nonlinear per-cell biases in transcript counts.

% 2. counting (reads → counts)
Counting starts from the paired sequences read from both ends of a fragment (paired reads) or the 3’ end sequences (single reads),
as well as the read’s or read pair’s cell barcode and – if available – its UMI barcode.
Classic counting uses specialized short read alignment tools like BOWTIE or STAR to match reads to a reference genome annotated with information about where genes start, end, and can be spliced.
For each known splicing configuration translating to a protein, the reads aligned to it are then counted, while collapsing the counts for reads with identical UMIs – if available (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]c).
Since only the resulting count matrix is needed for many kinds of downstream analysis, there have been advances in pseudo-alignment algorithms like Kallisto or Salmon, which directly count the reads.

% 3. preprocessing (counts → expressions)
% generally luecken2018: https://www.embopress.org/doi/10.15252/msb.20188746
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:batch-effect,location=right,title={Batch effect correction reduces biases between data gathered in multiple sequencer runs. Cells from well-normalized data will not only have similar expression profiles to cells from the same run.}]
\externalfigure[batch-effect-correction.svg][width=.5\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

In order to obtain comparable quantities, raw counts have to be preprocessed to correct for various technical biases.
Commonly one starts by eliminating rows and columns from the count matrix that contain uncharacteristically low counts: No gene or cell is likely to only contain a single read.
Another common preprocessing step is normalization:
correction for common cell variation caused by known processes such as the cell cycle, if one isn’t interested in studying this process.
If multiple runs of the sequencer were used to obtain the full data, there will be biases per run that can be reduced by using a dedicated batch effect correction algori\-thm (see \infig[fig:batch-effect]).
Preprocessing can also involve scaling count values using a per-cell function derived from the aforementioned spike-ins.
Usually, count data is logarithmized, as in log-space, distances represent log-fold changes, the usual way to compare expression level.
Many downstream analyses expect this property, as they model normal distributions or use a distance metric designed for data with no mean–variance dependency. \cite[brennecke2013,eling2018]
A final common preprocessing step is reduction of the data by discarding genes with low variability that are assumed to contribute little to the data’s variability (highly variable gene selection).
\TODO[mention quality control as part of counting and preprocessing]

% 4. analysis: uncover dynamics, heterogeneity structure, feature importance and role
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:waddington,location=left,title={Analysis: Cells differentiate smoothly, in stochasically diverging paths, and not always in the same general direction.}]
%https://anatomypubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/dvdy.24678
\externalfigure[https://anatomypubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/cms/asset/aafdf349-9324-496d-822d-183edae5d947/dvdy24678-fig-0001-m.jpg][width=.5\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

\TODO[Figure: better use linear discrimination using marker genes? or smoothly diverging developmental markers?]
With unwanted sources of noise and variability removed or reduced, the data can be analyzed.
Analysis of single cell data focuses on heterogeneity, as its presence separates single cell data from bulk data.
The kind of heterogeneity to uncover depends on the kind of data available:
the simplest case is discrimination between treatments or other conditions.
A possible goal is to find a small number of marker genes able to distinguish the same condition with a less expensive targeted approach.
Methods performing this search are called differential gene expression analyses.
Another source of heterogeneity is the composition of the studied tissue.
Tumors as well as many kinds of healthy tissue are composed of cells fulfilling different roles.
Traditionally, cells were thought of exhibiting a discrete cell type, which still applies to cells that are dramatically specialized for their roles.
There are also tissues consisting of less discrete cell types, like the pluripotent stem cells from which all cells derive.
During their path of development, cells usually get increasingly specialized, but the reverse direction (dedifferentiation) can also be observed (see \infig[fig:waddington]).
Furthermore, developing cells do not necessarily follow all steps of differentiation in the same order or magnitude.
This results in cells temporarily or sometimes permanently stuck as intermediate types.
While marker genes for certain cell roles or developmental steps exist, this complex and gradual nature of most tissues requires more sophisticated analysis and visualization.
Further analyses can also depend on the availability of other kinds of data.
For example an indication of the direction in which cells differentiate are RNA velocities, which can be reconstructed from the relation of spliced and unspliced mRNA \cite[manno2018].

% 5. visualization (results often too complex to understand as numbers)
% talk about dim red here and the fact that it’s hard to reason about how the input features relate back to output – make path for GR coming to the rescue
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:embeddings, title={Four increasingly complex two dimensional embeddings of the same cells. The differentiating cells are colored by age in days. (a) Two genes, even carefully selected markers, are not able to separate the differentiation paths (b) A PCA is able to differentiate clusters, but not like (c) diffusion maps, bifurcations and smooth transitions. (d) UMAPs use machine learning, allowing here to flatten multiple kinds of differences into a 2D plot, as in this case both bifurcation events present in the data.}]
\externalfigure[dim-red.pdf][width=\textwidth]
%\input{imgs/dim-red.tikz}
\stopplacefigure

The complexity of the heterogeneity being uncovered not only reflects in analysis methods but also the visualizations of their results.
Complex developmental hierarchies and other relations in the data need to be faithfully represented.
This commonly involves laying out points representing the cells in a flat plane while trying to maintain relationships such as developmental closeness (see \infig[fig:embeddings]).
This process and its end result is called embedding.
And the more complex a process is, the more likely it is that this process contains steps that cannot be traced back.
This presents a challenge for one’s visualizations and any insights one wants to derive from it:
with no ability to validate the embedding, any arbitrary arrangement would be equally valid.
