\stopsubsection

The above sequence of steps should be a generic overview of scRNA-Seq analysis able to serve as a framework in which to place my specific contributions.
While I worked on every in-silico section \TODO[better term] of the process except for alignment, my contributions are focused on the steps after counting.
To provide further context for my work, I would like to contrast the ways people used to work before and after the creation of my tools.

\startsubsection[title={The old way}]
% People often need to cobble together their own pipelines and use default parameters without knowing what they do. Existing methods are not focused on interoperability. Mention specifically the flaws fixed by what I do:
Without publicly available analysis frameworks, the most common way to operate was writing scripts that invoked command line tools.
Each step invoked reading text files from a file system, processing them, and writing them back to disk.
% - pre-scanpy: Integrated solution of everything helps save time and eliminate invalid states (type theory: make invalid states unrepresentable)
%               scran+Seurat are more opinionated and offer less choice (host code problem).
%               seurat has very seurat-specific object, scanpy’s anndata is generic
This process involved long run times due to slow reading and writing, high storage space requirements and little resilience to errors.
File corruption introduced e.\,g. via unreliable access to network storage could not be detected in text files, which lack checksums and other resilience precautions.
All those problems can be circumvented by a sufficient investment of effort and time.
In practive, the lack of frameworks led to very application-specific code that was hard to generalize and share.
Redundant solving of problems already solved elsewhere is not only wasteful, but also error prone.
No scRNA-Seq analysis framework in Python existed, despite more and more people in the field working with machine learning algorithms implemented in Python.
Existing tools in R were opinionated and offered little extensibility, limiting their scope of application.
Their performance was inconsistent and often not sufficient for upcoming large datasets from the upcoming microfluidic protocols.
Similarly, their code quality and error reporting varied greatly.
For example, many common differential expression tools used to simply process data being put in, with no errors reported in the case of non-converging models or other detectable degenerate cases \cite[luecken2018].
\TODO[Check if *this* Lücken citation says that]
Furthermore, no data structures and file exchange formats suitable for scRNA-Seq existed.
Existing workflows dealt with raw matrices and data frames that needed to be kept in sync manually across filtering steps, increasing the potential for error.
Furthermore, reproducibility was hard to guarantee in the presence of nondeterministic methods and a multitude of tool versions with subtly different defaults and behavior.
% - pre-destiny: DM creation in R
In the R landscape, the Bioconductor project provided tools for preprocessing, and basic analysis, but little in the way of embeddings.
The only available option for diffusion map embedding at the time did not offer features necessary for scRNA-Seq analysis and was impractically slow.
\TODO[can’t introduce PCA and DMs here…]
% - pre-GR: differential expression and trendsceek
No dimension reduction method except for the simplistic PCA provides a way to relate embedding location back to input features.
The existing and popular differential expression analysis requires discrete partitioning of the data, which is not well defined for data containing gradual transition of gene expression profiles between cells.
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={The new way}]
% - Through frameworks, data interchange formats and containers we get interoperability and reproducibility.
Today, Python and R have viable frameworks for scRNA-Seq analysis based on annotated expression matrix data structures.
These data structures allow keeping gene and cell annotations and graphs in sync with the expression matrix while filtering and concatenating data.
In R, the landscape is more fragmented than in Python:
The Bioconductor ecosystem provides annotated matrix data structures and tools for mainly preprocessing scRNA-Seq data.
Seurat provides opinionated preprocessing, analysis and plotting routines but is not integrated into the Bioconductor ecosystem.
Its data structure is Seurat-specific and needs conversion to the more generic Bioconductor data structures.
Standalone tools often support Bioconductor data structures or just plain matrices and data frames.
%reproducibility
In both language environments, reproducibility seems to be largely possible.
Container solutions that capture a whole environment of libraries of specific versions are common.
Frameworks support methods to set random seeds to get identical output of nondeterministic processes for consistent visualizations.
%anndata, scanpy
In Python, many standalone tools operate on the generic AnnData data structure.
It was developed alongside Scanpy, which is now in widespread international use and being continuously expanded with new algorithms.
Scanpy contains wrappers around third party tools which are often slim as they already directly operate on AnnData objects.
\TODO[already said above, deduplicate]
AnnData supports reading and writing from and to several language agnostic interchange formats.
It was the first annotated matrix data structure to support sparse expression matrices and therefore up to millions of cells.
It provides its own format that supports disk backing for operations on data too large to be performed in memory, sparse or dense.
% - serving all MOs: iterative with biologists, manual coding, let it be done as-a-service
Scanpy lends itself to be used interactively to quickly iterate on analyses with close feedback from biologists.
It also supports being used as a Python library to back visualization tools, web servers and traditional file-based processing pipelines.
% - good error reporting / software quality important for good science (Avoid garbage-in garbage-out)
% - principle of many eyes: less bugs in shared code
The number of users of a framework or tool contributes to its quality and therefore the quality of studies relying on it.
Bigger frameworks encompassing many tools have an easier time to test several permutations of their functionality than each tool would be able to when standing alone.
More people reporting errors and special use cases contribute to the robustness of the frameworks towards those problems.
%destiny, gr
New embeddings have been developed, none of which are able to relate embedding dimensions back to the input genes.
